WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.375 --> 00:00:01.550
[shoggoth growling]

00:00:01.550 --> 00:00:05.835
This is a shoggoth from
HBO's "Lovecraft Country."

00:00:05.835 --> 00:00:08.540
But on set, it was a different story.

00:00:08.540 --> 00:00:11.570
Believe it or not, these
puppet heads and arms

00:00:11.570 --> 00:00:15.430
made the monster scarier on
screen, but that's not all.

00:00:15.430 --> 00:00:17.980
From keeping the monster's mouth open

00:00:17.980 --> 00:00:20.470
to treating its anatomy like a weapon

00:00:20.470 --> 00:00:22.290
to what kind of prosthetic head

00:00:22.290 --> 00:00:24.870
the stand-ins would hold up on set,

00:00:24.870 --> 00:00:28.810
there were a lot of choices the
"Lovecraft" team had to make

00:00:28.810 --> 00:00:33.630
to make sure the show's scariest
monsters terrified viewers.

00:00:33.630 --> 00:00:37.687
Warning: Spoilers ahead
for "Lovecraft Country."

00:00:38.540 --> 00:00:41.630
When HP Lovecraft wrote
about the shoggoths

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:43.780
in "At the Mountains of Madness,"

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:46.338
he called them "indescribable."

00:00:46.338 --> 00:00:48.180
Not much to go off of.

00:00:48.180 --> 00:00:50.405
So the "Lovecraft" team brought in

00:00:50.405 --> 00:00:55.120
VFX supervisor Grant Walker
and his team at Framestore

00:00:55.120 --> 00:00:59.570
to make the indescribable
into something tangibly scary.

00:00:59.570 --> 00:01:02.278
The VFX team worked off sketches,

00:01:02.278 --> 00:01:05.353
references, and concept art of the monster

00:01:05.353 --> 00:01:08.130
created by Framestore's art department.

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.762
When Grant received the concept art,

00:01:10.762 --> 00:01:13.153
many prominent features of the shoggoths

00:01:13.153 --> 00:01:15.221
were already established.

00:01:15.221 --> 00:01:17.970
For instance, that they had four limbs

00:01:17.970 --> 00:01:21.060
as well as these tiny T. rex-like arms.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:23.080
They did early animation tests

00:01:23.080 --> 00:01:25.390
in a step called previsualization

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:28.930
to see how the monsters would
look and move in scenes.

00:01:28.930 --> 00:01:31.440
Framestore's animators couldn't really get

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:34.890
into the CGI nitty-gritty
until they had an idea

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:37.660
of what the shoggoths would
look like in the scenes.

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:40.900
And they could do that thanks
to these stunt performers.

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.880
Stand-ins are necessary for
both the actors and animators,

00:01:44.880 --> 00:01:47.730
allowing something
terrifying to be on camera

00:01:47.730 --> 00:01:49.700
that isn't actually there yet.

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:52.100
They give the cast something to react to,

00:01:52.100 --> 00:01:53.950
whether they're being eaten alive

00:01:53.950 --> 00:01:56.270
or just screaming their heads off.

00:01:56.270 --> 00:01:59.130
By leaping off a ledge
and wrapping his arms

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:00.937
under this cop's armpits,

00:02:00.937 --> 00:02:03.295
this stand-in can show Framestore

00:02:03.295 --> 00:02:05.936
where to place the shoggoth's sharp arms

00:02:05.936 --> 00:02:08.850
that pierce straight
through the actor's chest.

00:02:08.850 --> 00:02:12.840
But it's not so easy to be
convincingly scary on set,

00:02:12.840 --> 00:02:16.567
especially with these big
googly eyes and missing teeth.

00:02:16.567 --> 00:02:18.091
Grant Walker: When the actors
first saw the shoggoth,

00:02:18.091 --> 00:02:20.401
I think they got all their
laughing out of their system

00:02:20.401 --> 00:02:22.371
so that they could perform on set.

00:02:22.371 --> 00:02:25.008
Narrator: And the stand-ins
make Framestore's job

00:02:25.008 --> 00:02:28.990
much easier by identifying
characteristics like shadows,

00:02:28.990 --> 00:02:31.450
which make them seem more realistic.

00:02:31.450 --> 00:02:35.040
And it is important to be
as precise as possible.

00:02:35.040 --> 00:02:38.560
In this shot, where a shoggoth
stands on its hind legs

00:02:38.560 --> 00:02:41.210
in front of Tic and Leti, this stuntperson

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:45.000
had to hold this prosthetic
head about nine feet high,

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:47.250
which is the height of the black shoggoth

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.030
as estimated by Grant.

00:02:49.030 --> 00:02:51.560
Given the shoggoth's strength and speed,

00:02:51.560 --> 00:02:55.660
even the best stuntperson can't
imitate every single move,

00:02:55.660 --> 00:02:58.370
but they do help track the exact timing

00:02:58.370 --> 00:03:00.730
and space of where the monster would be,

00:03:00.730 --> 00:03:04.540
allowing for a better and
scarier CGI performance.

00:03:04.540 --> 00:03:08.640
Plus, the actors will be
looking directly into its face,

00:03:08.640 --> 00:03:12.030
as opposed to accidentally
looking up into the sky.

00:03:12.030 --> 00:03:14.700
Grant had many choices
for how to represent

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:17.210
the monster's massive size on set.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:19.810
First, he and the crew tried to stack

00:03:19.810 --> 00:03:22.040
two stand-ins on top of each other,

00:03:22.040 --> 00:03:25.835
but they found they could more
easily cast the same shadow

00:03:25.835 --> 00:03:28.682
by designing a prosthetic head and arms.

00:03:28.682 --> 00:03:31.287
Grant: I took the initial
model of the shoggoth,

00:03:31.287 --> 00:03:33.520
and I took it in its neutral pose,

00:03:33.520 --> 00:03:35.957
and I chopped its body off.

00:03:35.957 --> 00:03:37.450
And I left its head where it was,

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:39.160
and I left its arms where it was,

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:41.240
and I figured out how they would hold them

00:03:41.240 --> 00:03:45.120
to hold them in the approximate
stance of a shoggoth's pose

00:03:45.120 --> 00:03:47.790
based on all fours and
then stood up on two legs.

00:03:47.790 --> 00:03:50.630
And so I knew that if there
was a 6-foot stunt guy,

00:03:50.630 --> 00:03:51.710
he would hold it up here.

00:03:51.710 --> 00:03:54.890
Narrator: Now, not every
shot had a stuntperson in it.

00:03:54.890 --> 00:03:57.970
Sometimes the monster was just too fast

00:03:57.970 --> 00:03:59.750
for a stuntperson to mimic.

00:03:59.750 --> 00:04:02.080
Framestore was given both the shots

00:04:02.080 --> 00:04:04.700
with the stand-ins and identical versions

00:04:04.700 --> 00:04:07.020
of those shots with just the backdrop

00:04:07.020 --> 00:04:10.030
and no reference, known as a clean plate.

00:04:10.030 --> 00:04:13.410
Animators then had to
place the CGI shoggoths

00:04:13.410 --> 00:04:14.640
into their scenes.

00:04:14.640 --> 00:04:17.170
What a stand-in couldn't
communicate to Grant

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:19.980
was how many eyes to put on the monsters

00:04:19.980 --> 00:04:22.405
or how sharp to make their teeth,

00:04:22.405 --> 00:04:24.130
so it was up to Framestore

00:04:24.130 --> 00:04:26.410
to get creative with the details.

00:04:26.410 --> 00:04:29.350
They made the shoggoth's skin translucent,

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:32.350
so you can see its entire inner structure

00:04:32.350 --> 00:04:33.920
if you shine a light on it.

00:04:33.920 --> 00:04:36.200
Since the shoggoths live underground,

00:04:36.200 --> 00:04:39.010
they looked to real animals
that live in the dark.

00:04:39.010 --> 00:04:42.670
After looking at naked mole
rats and deep-sea fish,

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:46.070
they saw their skin tended
to be somewhat see-through.

00:04:46.070 --> 00:04:47.990
This physical feature played into

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.040
the first episode's storyline.

00:04:50.040 --> 00:04:52.940
When the characters shined
a light on the shoggoths,

00:04:52.940 --> 00:04:54.850
it not only changed the appearance

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:58.000
of the monsters' skin,
but also repelled them.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:00.100
But this presented a challenge,

00:05:00.100 --> 00:05:02.960
as see-through skin is hard to render.

00:05:02.960 --> 00:05:06.630
So they actually chose not
to make the skin see-through

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.890
until the compositing
process, the final element

00:05:09.890 --> 00:05:12.720
when everything comes
together in animation.

00:05:12.720 --> 00:05:14.800
And because the skin was see-through,

00:05:14.800 --> 00:05:17.170
Grant had to pay extra-special attention

00:05:17.170 --> 00:05:21.330
to its inner details, like
veins or tiny bits of jaw

00:05:21.330 --> 00:05:23.910
that connect teeth to
the mouth and the skull.

00:05:23.910 --> 00:05:27.140
Normally, those just
serve a practical purpose,

00:05:27.140 --> 00:05:31.160
but here they also had to
look anatomically correct.

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:34.140
The shoggoths weren't
just covered in skin.

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.110
They had eyeballs everywhere.

00:05:37.110 --> 00:05:39.785
Like the skin, all 40 eyeballs

00:05:39.785 --> 00:05:42.335
needed to look sickly and diseased,

00:05:42.335 --> 00:05:44.535
so they looked to the animal kingdom.

00:05:44.535 --> 00:05:46.840
Grant: We didn't want
the eyes to be beautiful.

00:05:46.840 --> 00:05:49.530
No one wants to look deep
into the soul of a shoggoth.

00:05:49.530 --> 00:05:52.030
We wanted to look at horrible dark eyes,

00:05:52.030 --> 00:05:53.780
like the black eyes of a shark.

00:05:53.780 --> 00:05:55.810
Narrator: When designing a scary creature,

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:58.460
you have to make sure the scary features

00:05:58.460 --> 00:06:00.110
still function properly.

00:06:00.110 --> 00:06:02.250
For example, the white shoggoth

00:06:02.250 --> 00:06:04.875
featured in the first
episode of "Lovecraft"

00:06:04.875 --> 00:06:06.258
had black eyes.

00:06:06.258 --> 00:06:08.800
Grant was afraid that
if they were too black,

00:06:08.800 --> 00:06:11.840
the audience wouldn't know
where the shoggoth monsters

00:06:11.840 --> 00:06:13.520
were looking at a given moment.

00:06:13.520 --> 00:06:15.263
So instead of using the pupils

00:06:15.263 --> 00:06:17.470
to see what direction it was looking in,

00:06:17.470 --> 00:06:21.000
the audience could instead
follow the surrounding skin

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:24.360
and this moving layer of mucusy membrane

00:06:24.360 --> 00:06:25.930
on top of its eye.

00:06:25.930 --> 00:06:27.550
Being a visual effects artist

00:06:27.550 --> 00:06:29.620
involves lots of experimentation.

00:06:29.620 --> 00:06:31.440
This is how Framestore landed on

00:06:31.440 --> 00:06:33.847
an important decision about the shoggoth:

00:06:33.847 --> 00:06:36.900
keeping its mouth open
almost all the time.

00:06:36.900 --> 00:06:39.770
Early on, Grant looked at
what one of the shoggoths

00:06:39.770 --> 00:06:41.850
would look like with its mouth closed.

00:06:41.850 --> 00:06:43.720
Grant: It looked like Kermit the Frog.

00:06:43.720 --> 00:06:45.380
Narrator: Sure, Kermit is OK

00:06:45.380 --> 00:06:47.770
for a temporary stand-in on set,

00:06:47.770 --> 00:06:50.440
but not a terrifying finished product.

00:06:50.440 --> 00:06:53.630
So they kept the mouth
open whenever they could.

00:06:53.630 --> 00:06:55.650
Grant: The creature's head is 80% mouth.

00:06:55.650 --> 00:06:57.530
Narrator: When it came to what's inside

00:06:57.530 --> 00:07:00.270
the creature's mouth,
Grant made it scarier

00:07:00.270 --> 00:07:03.000
by turning to some classic horror monsters

00:07:03.000 --> 00:07:04.190
that have terrified him.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:07.210
The tongue was turned
into a multipronged one

00:07:07.210 --> 00:07:10.690
inspired by "The Thing,"
and he drew inspiration

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:13.578
for the sharp fangs from Pennywise

00:07:13.578 --> 00:07:16.550
and studied animal
predators for inspiration.

00:07:16.550 --> 00:07:18.691
Grant: We looked at sharks,

00:07:18.691 --> 00:07:20.292
who have got many rows of teeth,

00:07:20.292 --> 00:07:22.830
but the top jaw is not
connected to the cranium,

00:07:22.830 --> 00:07:24.877
and a humanoid jaw is
connected to the cranium,

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:26.582
whereas a shark's, it protrudes,

00:07:26.582 --> 00:07:28.627
so you can actually turn the teeth out,

00:07:28.627 --> 00:07:30.940
and it makes the really
wide-stretching jaw.

00:07:30.940 --> 00:07:33.811
Like, the teeth start pointing
at what it's going to eat,

00:07:33.811 --> 00:07:35.260
which is terrifying.

00:07:35.260 --> 00:07:38.680
Narrator: The mouth was
filled with 4,000 teeth,

00:07:38.680 --> 00:07:41.278
which is no easy feat to animate,

00:07:41.278 --> 00:07:44.710
as CGI teeth naturally
want to start intersecting

00:07:44.710 --> 00:07:46.020
when you put them together.

00:07:46.020 --> 00:07:48.530
This would ruin any frightening illusion,

00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:51.530
so Framestore used a
specific rigging method

00:07:51.530 --> 00:07:52.855
that, according to Grant,

00:07:52.855 --> 00:07:55.380
allowed them to nudge the teeth around

00:07:55.380 --> 00:07:57.430
and keep them in the right place.

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:00.238
Part of what makes the monster so scary

00:08:00.238 --> 00:08:01.712
is that the VFX team

00:08:01.712 --> 00:08:04.410
turned parts of their bodies into weapons.

00:08:04.410 --> 00:08:07.530
This decision came from
a conversation Grant had

00:08:07.530 --> 00:08:10.100
with "Lovecraft" creator Misha Green.

00:08:10.100 --> 00:08:12.060
Grant: Misha described it I think once

00:08:12.060 --> 00:08:14.560
as, "It's a Swiss Army knife of weapons."

00:08:14.560 --> 00:08:17.177
And so every limb, every point,

00:08:17.177 --> 00:08:19.838
every part of the anatomy
has to have a purpose.

00:08:19.838 --> 00:08:23.059
And if you don't see it use
that purpose on the screen,

00:08:23.059 --> 00:08:24.330
it kind of gets forgotten.

00:08:24.330 --> 00:08:27.380
Narrator: The teeth were
built for maximum bloodshed.

00:08:27.380 --> 00:08:31.370
Originally, Grant said they
tried to make the teeth spin,

00:08:31.370 --> 00:08:33.820
but it pushed the limits of plausibility.

00:08:33.820 --> 00:08:36.720
Instead it was scarier
to just make the teeth

00:08:36.720 --> 00:08:40.960
clamp down so fast that they
could liquidize a human head.

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:44.200
Now, remember those tiny
arms from early designs?

00:08:44.200 --> 00:08:46.490
They were designed with sharp claws

00:08:46.490 --> 00:08:48.680
and only really look like tiny arms

00:08:48.680 --> 00:08:51.510
until they're used to cut
right through somebody.

00:08:51.510 --> 00:08:54.110
You can also see in some early designs

00:08:54.110 --> 00:08:57.050
that a shoggoth tail
looked more like a stub.

00:08:57.050 --> 00:08:58.663
When animators extended it,

00:08:58.663 --> 00:09:01.462
they also added a bladelike tip to it.

00:09:01.462 --> 00:09:04.170
But it wasn't just how
the shoggoths looked

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:05.703
that made them so scary.

00:09:05.703 --> 00:09:08.030
The right movement is key for making

00:09:08.030 --> 00:09:10.870
a CGI creature truly terrifying.

00:09:10.870 --> 00:09:13.930
The shoggoths are fast,
and Grant and his team

00:09:13.930 --> 00:09:17.150
had the freedom to figure
out exactly how fast.

00:09:17.150 --> 00:09:20.100
Once again, they looked
to the animal kingdom.

00:09:20.100 --> 00:09:22.180
Grant and his team initially mimicked

00:09:22.180 --> 00:09:23.707
the movement of gorillas,

00:09:23.707 --> 00:09:26.490
but gorillas are slower
than what they wanted.

00:09:26.490 --> 00:09:28.148
So they looked to the cheetah,

00:09:28.148 --> 00:09:30.064
but that cat is too lean.

00:09:30.064 --> 00:09:33.420
So, instead, they based
the movement off a lion,

00:09:33.420 --> 00:09:36.100
a fast runner and a heavy predator.

00:09:36.100 --> 00:09:38.750
Grant even matched the
shoggoth's run cycle

00:09:38.750 --> 00:09:42.730
with a lion's average
speed of 50 miles per hour.

00:09:42.730 --> 00:09:46.180
Even the smallest detail
didn't go overlooked.

00:09:46.180 --> 00:09:48.240
As the shoggoths leap into action,

00:09:48.240 --> 00:09:51.920
they also leave behind
puddles of nasty drool.

00:09:51.920 --> 00:09:53.300
Grant: It's got a huge mouth.

00:09:53.300 --> 00:09:55.460
We want to make it scary and disgusting.

00:09:55.460 --> 00:09:57.910
Narrator: Because it is
so difficult to control

00:09:57.910 --> 00:09:59.732
and there is so much of it,

00:09:59.732 --> 00:10:03.820
drool is one of the hardest
elements to recreate in CGI.

00:10:03.820 --> 00:10:06.350
So like hair, water, and clothes,

00:10:06.350 --> 00:10:08.068
it needed to be simulated,

00:10:08.068 --> 00:10:09.997
which in 3D animation just means

00:10:09.997 --> 00:10:14.057
a computer calculated how
something, in this case drool,

00:10:14.057 --> 00:10:17.060
would realistically behave
in certain conditions.

00:10:17.060 --> 00:10:19.920
There also had to be
multiple types of drool

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:21.653
for different scenarios,

00:10:21.653 --> 00:10:23.661
like drool running down the mouth,

00:10:23.661 --> 00:10:25.798
drool that stuck to its teeth,

00:10:25.798 --> 00:10:29.500
and drool that stuck on
other surfaces like the road.

00:10:29.500 --> 00:10:31.770
And nothing says gross and scary

00:10:31.770 --> 00:10:34.420
like drool filled with blood.

00:10:34.420 --> 00:10:36.390
Being an animator doesn't mean

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:38.680
you sit behind a computer all day.

00:10:38.680 --> 00:10:42.050
To make this complicated
shot of the black shoggoth,

00:10:42.050 --> 00:10:45.093
Grant filmed himself
acting like the monster.

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:49.570
Grant: When we were figuring it all out,

00:10:49.570 --> 00:10:51.810
we knew it was at close
proximity to the cameras.

00:10:51.810 --> 00:10:53.370
Narrator: Grant practiced biting

00:10:53.370 --> 00:10:55.400
and shredding a fake arm.

00:10:55.400 --> 00:10:58.370
Then the stuntperson
replicated those moves.

00:10:58.370 --> 00:11:00.130
Grant: And then they add their extra

00:11:00.130 --> 00:11:01.893
flourish of performance to it.

